Title: "Natural Numbers"
Id: "acc9e650-ac98-4a0b-821a-5bc440a9388e"


Text: "The natural numbers are the numbers of counting: zero, one, two, and so
       on without end. This chapter builds them from the ground already laid.
       First they are characterized abstractly by the Peano axioms -- a starting
       point, a successor operation, and the principle of induction -- and then
       a concrete set of naturals is assembled from the sets of the earlier
       chapter, with addition defined by recursion."
Id: "3079172c-5778-44bd-9391-60ce0f0b12e7"


SectionTitle: "The Peano axioms"
Id: "08e79326-5fb2-4d0e-8f35-1b437d85b38b"


Text: "The abstract naturals are any set carrying a distinguished element $0$
       and a successor operation obeying the Peano axioms: zero is not a
       successor, successor is injective, and the set is generated from zero by
       repeated succession. This last condition is the principle of mathematical
       induction, and it is what makes the naturals rigid enough to be unique up
       to relabeling."
Id: "fbe31158-8082-4444-82f6-a8049d0bb921"


[\abstract.naturals]
Defines: N ::= (X, 0, succ)
means:
. X is \set
. 0 "in" X
. succ is (? "in" X) -> (? "in" X)
satisfies:
. 0 "in" X
. let: n "in" X
  then: succ(n) "in" X
. not:
  . exists: n "in" X
    suchThat: succ(n) = 0
. let: n, m "in" X
  then:
  . if: succ(n) = succ(m)
    then: n = m
Documented:
. called: "abstract naturals"
. description: "An abstract natural number system is a set $X$ with an element
                $0$ and a successor map, satisfying the Peano axioms: $0$ is not
                a successor, distinct numbers have distinct successors, and $X$
                is exactly what is reached from $0$ by successors. Any two such
                systems are isomorphic."
Id: "7d1762ec-ee47-4d7d-86b9-cc0150b06bd0"


SectionTitle: "Zero, successor, and the naturals"
Id: "9f2d601b-cdb2-4cbc-a213-a70d5b0dc291"


Text: "We now name the individual pieces. The number zero is the base case, the
       one natural number that starts the whole sequence."
Id: "4154a694-b3ae-4567-be04-de74e8c44f58"


[\natural.0]
Declares: 0 is \natural
Documented:
. written: "0"
. description: "The natural number zero, the base of the number system. It is
                the unique natural number that is not the successor of any
                other."
Id: "812550b8-d357-4564-96de-1053d332b817"


Text: "The successor operation produces the next number after a given one.
       Counting is nothing but the repeated application of successor starting
       from zero."
Id: "e86827d5-dce2-458d-b1c6-fd2e2036f737"


[\natural.succ(n_)]
Declares: succ(n_) is (? is \natural) -> (? is \natural)
Documented:
. called: "successor of $n?$"
. written: "n?+\!\!+"
. description: "The successor of a natural number $n$ is the natural number
                immediately after it, informally $n + 1$. Every natural number
                other than zero is the successor of exactly one natural number."
Id: "ffa1fbbf-8be1-4fd7-b759-5f39b30840e7"


Text: "A natural number is then defined to be either zero or the successor of
       some natural number. This recursive description is the definitional form
       of the induction principle: it says the naturals are built up from zero,
       one successor at a time, and nothing else."
Id: "6e4c72e5-cace-4331-bfc4-149a06321d20"


[\natural]
Defines: n
satisfies:
. oneOf:
  . n = \natural.0
  . exists: m is \natural
    suchThat: n = \natural.succ(m)
Requires:
. definition: \natural.0 is \natural
. definition: \natural.succ is (? is \natural) -> (? is \natural)
Enables:
. capability: x_++ :=> \natural.succ(x_)
. capability: x_ + y_ :=> x_ \.natural.+./ y_
. capability: x_ +_+--+*' y_ :=> x_ \.natural.+./ y_
Documented:
. called: "natural"
. description: "A natural number is either $0$ or the successor of a natural
                number, and these two cases are exclusive. Every natural number
                is reached from $0$ by finitely many successors, which is the
                basis for defining operations on the naturals by recursion."
Id: "d88e480c-de0e-4e48-92b0-54bd232a346e"


Text: "Collecting all the natural numbers into a single set gives $\mathbb{N}$,
       the set on which the arithmetic below operates."
Id: "709b99f1-7e8b-4c6b-a94d-4ac6a166c6d1"


[\naturals.set]
Declares: N := \set@{n_ : n_ is \natural}
Documented:
. written: "\mathbb{N}"
. description: "The set $\mathbb{N}$ of all natural numbers, gathered from the
                individual naturals by set-builder notation. It is the domain of
                the addition and order defined in this chapter."
Id: "114560d1-ffbf-4c9a-ba3f-243483ccfa2b"


Text: "The abstract characterization and the concrete set fit together: the set
       $\mathbb{N}$, with its zero and successor, is a model of the abstract
       naturals, and may be coerced to its underlying set when only the set is
       needed."
Id: "74acea45-65a7-4623-bb26-7abfb866fcde"


[\naturals]
Declares: N ::= (X, 0, succ) is \abstract.naturals
expresses:
. X := \naturals.set
. 0 := \natural.0
. succ := \natural.succ
Enables:
. relation:
  to: Y := X is \set
  represents: \\coercion
Documented:
. called: "naturals"
. description: "The natural numbers packaged as a concrete instance of the
                abstract naturals: the set $\mathbb{N}$ with the element $0$ and
                the successor map. This exhibits $\mathbb{N}$ as a genuine Peano
                system."
Id: "06fc295f-367d-4069-9f52-817e2894e3eb"


Text: "The nonzero naturals are called *positive*. They are exactly the
       successors, the numbers $1, 2, 3, \dots$ obtained by starting the count
       one step past zero."
Id: "b64bdab5-e173-420e-9328-dd753b00bc74"


[\(positive)::natural]
Refines: n
satisfies: n != \natural.0
Documented:
. adjective: "positive"
Id: "0fd2f7cb-f348-4982-a873-40e3e4e87c19"


SectionTitle: "Equality and addition"
Id: "9af7aad0-ff03-4ed7-9c1a-6011f01b25e8"


Text: "Equality of naturals can be described recursively, mirroring their
       recursive construction: two naturals are equal when they are both zero or
       are the successors of equal naturals."
Id: "e495d676-9b45-4b08-87b9-da31ff49eb6e"


[n \.natural.=./ m]
States:
when: n, m is \natural
that:
. if: n = 0
  then: m = 0
. if: n != 0
  then:
  . exists: k is \natural
    suchThat: n = \natural.succ(k)
Documented:
. written: "n? = m?"
Id: "3c49908d-060c-44c3-8f68-763b227e93d4"


Text: "Addition is the first arithmetic operation, and it is defined by
       recursion on the second argument: adding zero changes nothing, and adding
       a successor is the successor of the sum. From this definition all the
       familiar laws of addition can be proved by induction."
Id: "4bdc5f2a-4b93-4d8e-b14a-d8dbec7dfb4f"


[n_ \.natural.+./ m_]
Declares: n_ + m_ is \binary.operation:on{\naturals.set}
expresses:
. piecewise:
  if: n_ = 0
  then: n_ + m_ := m_
  else:
  . let:
    . k is \natural
    . n_ := \natural.succ(k)
    then: n_ + m_ := \natural.succ(n_ + k)
Documented:
. written: "n_? + m_?"
. description: "Addition of natural numbers, defined by recursion: $0 + m = m$,
                and the sum with a successor is the successor of the sum. This
                recursive definition determines $n + m$ for all naturals and
                underlies the properties of arithmetic."
Id: "5fd063d9-a3bd-4549-b060-259a727afab2"
